behind the look of every tesla is the  vision of one man and no it's not elon  musk you may have become aware of franz  von holtzausen when he smashed a cyber  truck window by accident  oh my god well  maybe that was a little too hard and  then the same thing happened again  oh  man softening the truck with a  sledgehammer beforehand  certainly didn't help franz von holzen  is the lead designer at tesla arguably  one of the best in the world uh anyway  welcome fronts awesome to have you guys  designing is in his blood his father was  an industrial designer in connecticut  and franz followed in his footsteps  studying industrial design at syracuse  university however while working for his  dad an intern mentioned a design school  out in la which piqued his interest so  he left syracuse two years into his  studies and enrolled at the arts center  college of design he also spent time at  its sister campus in switzerland which  exposed him to european influences he  wanted a career that focused on his love  of cars he once said it was important  for him to design products that were not  just beautiful but useful to people  quote i kind of have this belief that  you can create something beautiful and  it can be very personal but is it really  serving us as a human race it may be  serving you as an outlet for your own  personal desires but if it's not  creating a function out of that as well  is it really design  in 1992 he joined volkswagen straight  out of school to work on a top secret  project redesigning the beetle the  concept one is all but forgotten today  but in 1994 people went crazy for the  new take on the retro design it looked a  lot simpler than the beetle of the 60s  vonholzen had an eye for minimalism  early in his career he realized the look  of a car could change the way people  felt about a brand the new beetle  brought back his sense of nostalgia and  helped reinvigorate a company in decline  soon other brands decided to go retro  chrysler with the pt cruiser the mini  cooper and ford with the fifth  generation mustang after nearly eight  years at vw he moved to general motors  where he led the design for pontiac  solstice and the saturn sky although  they didn't really do well sales-wise  they were seen as really cool roadsters  pure and fun but as it turned out  working at a big car company with a huge  design team wasn't his thing as he  couldn't make the kind of impact he knew  he could make von holzes put it this way  during an interview with ashley vance  for his biography on elon musk i knew i  didn't want to die there after five  years at gm mazda came calling van holtz  hasn't produced concept cars that  reinvigorated mazda or as vonholz says  and put it to vance we brought the zoom  zoom back into the look and feel of the  car  three years later in 2008 he left mazda  for tesla  a friend had told von holzen that tesla  was looking for chief designer he  reached out to musk's assistant mary  beth brown and eventually met musk for  an interview at spacex headquarters musk  liked what he saw and sold it as an  opportunity to leave a cushy job to work  for a company that could potentially  shape the future they took a drive in  the roadster and van holtz hasn't  thought to himself as he recalled to  vance i knew i could stay at mazda for  10 years and get very comfortable or  take a huge leap of faith at tesla there  was no history no baggage there was just  a vision of products that could change  the world who wouldn't want to be  involved with that he said most of his  colleagues thought he was crazy for  joining tesla it was super risky very  few car startups succeed back then musk  only gave tesla a 10 shot of surviving  musk hired van holtz thousands  specifically to design the model s he  didn't have much to work with just a few  sketches and a clay model he knew the  model s would need to be iconic this  would be the foundation for the entire  tesla line he once said it needed to  attract people like a moth to a flame he  designed the model s in a corner of  spacex's factory and reportedly worked  on the prototype in a tent to keep away  from prying eyes musk handed over the  creative reigns to van holtzen but he  did stipulate that the model s needed to  be cool like an aston martin or a  porsche it needed to have a touch screen  as iphone technology was now all the  rage and musk knew it would soon be  commonplace but a touchscreen had never  before been installed in a car and the  ipad was still years away  musk and von holzen also drove the  engineers crazy when they insisted on a  smooth clean look for the door handles  as in they only popped out when the door  had to be opened in the end they got  their way musk also insisted on making  the body of the model s out of aluminum  rather than steel to compensate for the  heavy battery pack except car companies  in north america had no experience  producing body panels made of aluminum  aluminum is prone to cracking when it's  bent it can also develop lines that can  make it difficult to hide with paint but  the tesla engineers got it done the year  von holtz has enjoyed tesla in 2008. the  company struggled financially in fact  tesla nearly went bankrupt on christmas  eve that's why von holtz has in  desperately wanted to show the model s  to the world to get people excited about  what the company had to offer in march  2009 just six months after von holzen  joined the team tesla unveiled a gray  model s and a media event the media fond  over it wired magazine described it as a  stunningly beautiful car few knew it at  the time but the model s was far from  being ready for the road unbeknownst to  reporters the car had the base structure  of a mercedes cls according to ashley  vance the mercedes cls was the  foundation they used when working on the  s despite the work that still had to be  done tesla achieved its goal of  convincing people that it could make  beautiful electric cars and compete with  the big players like audi mercedes and  bmw the model s was a commercial success  improved to doubters there was indeed a  market for evs  the original model s was delivered in  2012 but it doesn't show its age even 10  years later it still looks pretty modern  today the newer version of the s no  longer includes the nose cone at the  front amongst other changes that was  decorative not functional so they took  it away simplifying the look even more  van holtzen's motto from the start has  been simplicity without excess which has  been baked into every tesla since  including the model 3 and will be an  integral part of every tesla to come  if you dream of building incredible  products and would like to excel in math  and science in order to do so you can  join the 11 million people who have  signed up for brilliant brilliant is an  amazing tool for learning stem in a fun  hands-on way just as the tesla team is  using neural networks to achieve  self-driving you can delve into the  world of neural networks through  brilliance introduction to neural  networks course to help push your math  skills to the next level you can learn  problem-solving techniques with your  mathematical fundamentals course or  learn the key ideas of computer science  with their computer science fundamentals  course and no coding is required  brilliant is great whether you're in  high school university or if you're  already in the workforce there are no  tests and if you ever get stuck you can  click on view explanation to see where  you went wrong to get started for free  you can head to the custom link in my  description brilliant.org  newsthink in the first 200 of you to  sign up with my link will get 20 off  brilliance premium subscription which  gives you unlimited access to all the  courses  thanks for watching for newsthink i'm  cindy palm
 

